window.docContents[200].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Headline/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Headline":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security › Headline.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 177 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\n\n\nReduction bundles for the one-key PQXDH headline statementTrusted: the statement. This file defines the one-key KeyInd reduction bundles and boundedness predicates used by PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ (HeadlineDelta.lean) and the CLI security statements (Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElf.lean). Companion one-key first-message Auth reduction bundles are in Auth/Headline.lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.For each query budget b and b-bounded game adversary A, a reduction family packages the adversaries against GapSigEufCma, KemShCr, and GapDhWithSign constructed from A for the one-key random-oracle PQXDH handshake game (KeyInd1KeyRo.lean). Each reduction is represented as a first-order ReductionDsl syntax tree (CostedReduction or TwoStageCostedReduction) over the pure operation table pqAtomicOps and coin-only sampler table pqAtomicSamplers, so step cost, output block sizes, and worst-case query counts are checked syntactically.The two-stage KemShCr reduction runs both stages with fuel := b.q_total; because a b-bounded adversary makes at most b.q_total game queries in total, fuel is never exhausted and the fallback return value is unreachable. Syntactic bounds (KeyIndCostedReductionBounds) check both the uniform stepCostBudget S b over b.q_total handled queries and the online/random-oracle split budget stepCostBudgetSplit S b b.q_ro via CostBoundedByKind, while semantic compliance (KeyIndReductionQueryBounds) enforces the sharper dynamic caps q_sig = 2 * min S.N_spk_tot S.N_recv and q_ddh = gdhQueryBudget S b declared by KeyIndCostedReductionFamily.eval.\n\n\n\nReduction bundles\n\nstructure KeyIndReductionFamilyL51–56 ↗#Shallow reduction family mapping each b-bounded KeyIndAdversary S to adversaries against GapSigEufCma (sig), KemShCr (shcr), and GapDhWithSign (gdh).\n\nstructure KeyIndCostedReductionFamilyL58–67 ↗#Deep-embedded ReductionDsl syntax trees over pqAtomicOps and pqKeyIndCodec S for the GapSigEufCma (sig), KemShCr (shcr), and GapDhWithSign (gdh) reductions.\n\ndef KeyIndCostedReductionFamily.evalL69–89 ↗#Interpret a KeyIndCostedReductionFamily into a KeyIndReductionFamily at fuel := b.q_total with fallback false for shcr.\n\nCost, block-size, and query bounds\n\ndef keyIndQueryKindL93–97 ↗#Classifies KeyIndQuery into online queries (0, matching isKeyIndOnlineQuery) and offline .roQuery random-oracle queries (1) for per-kind step-cost accounting.\n\ndef keyIndKindCountsL99–103 ↗#Per-kind query caps for a b-bounded KeyIndAdversary: min b.q_on b.q_total online queries (0) and min b.q_ro b.q_total random-oracle queries (1).\n\nstructure KeyIndCostedReductionBoundsL105–141 ↗#Syntactic resource bounds for R: size-honesty of pqAtomicOps and pqAtomicSamplers, uniform (stepCostBudget) and online/random-oracle split (stepCostBudgetSplit) step-cost bounds, worst-case query bounds over b.q_total handled queries, and run-local output block-size bounds.\n\nstructure KeyIndReductionQueryBoundsL143–148 ↗#Semantic query compliance of R: for every budget b and b-bounded adversary A, the evaluated sig, shcr, and gdh adversaries satisfy their .Bounded query predicates.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Headline.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Headline/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Headline"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliRun/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliRun":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › CliRun.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the symbolic proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 188 of 495 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (17)def readPathdef pathCompsdef isUnmodelledPathComponentdef PathOkdef ancestorsdef blockingAncestor?def unitResultdef unmodelleddef writeFileHookdef writeStdoutHookdef strlenHookdef memrchrHookdef Utf8Okdef fromUtf8Hookdef durationSinceExactHookstructure CliHooksdef cliBcmpHook\n\n\n\nPaths\n\ndef readPathL194–197 ↗#The path argument (ptr, len) of an I/O function as an FsState.files key: one character per byte (Char.ofNat), as the engine reads a path of openat (readCString, ISA/Eval.lean).\n\ndef pathCompsL199–201 ↗#The components of path p: its characters split at / (List.splitOn). On a PathOk path they are the segments of the engine's canonical form (Arm64.normalizePath).\n\ndef isUnmodelledPathComponentL203–205 ↗#A component the specifications do not model: empty (a leading, trailing or doubled /, or the empty path), . or ...\n\ndef PathOkL207–216 ↗#The paths the I/O specifications model: relative and canonical (every component non-empty and neither . nor ..), no NUL byte (Rust's CString would reject it), and no component that fsView hides (stdioPaths, Run.lean), so every file a hook reads or writes is observable. The standard streams themselves are not in the file system (FsState.files): the loader opens file descriptors 0, 1, 2 as .stdio descriptors, and stdin's bytes are FsState.stdin. On such paths the engine's canonical lookup (FsState.files) agrees with the way Linux resolves them.\n\ndef ancestorsL218–222 ↗#The proper ancestors of p, shallowest first: \"a/b/c\" gives [\"a\", \"a/b\"]; a single component has none (its parent is the party's directory, which exists).\n\ndef blockingAncestor?L224–229 ↗#The first proper ancestor of p that is not a directory, with what is there: some (some (.file _)) (Linux: ENOTDIR) or some none (missing: ENOENT); none if every proper ancestor is a directory. This is Linux's walk of the path's directories (link_path_walk).\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 233–267): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.SignalSession.vecErr, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.vecEmptyOk, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.vecOk\n\ndef unitResultL269–272 ↗#Return Ok(()) (x0 := 0) or Err(IoError) (x0 := 1) of a Result<(), IoError> (measured on the compiled write_file / write_stdout: cset w0, ne).\n\ndef unmodelledL274–276 ↗#Stop with an .Exception (outcomeOf is then none): the hook's inputs are outside what it models.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 281–308): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.SignalSession.readFileHook\n\ndef writeFileHookL310–345 ↗#libsignal_cli_io::write_file(path: &str = (x0, x1), bytes: &[u8] = (x2, x3)) -> Result<(), IoError> (x0): 0-allocation C-ABI musl open/write/close/__errno_location loop (O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC | O_CLOEXEC, mode 0o666, < 4096-byte NUL-free stack path copy, EINTR retry on open and write, n == 0 treated as Err, close on every exit after open), i.e. create or truncate, then write all of bytes.* .Exception if the path or the bytes are not readable, or (after the length / interior-NUL check) the path is not PathOk;* path byte length ≥ cliPathMaxBytes (4096, PATH_MAX) or containing an interior 0 byte: Err (unitResult false, before openat and before emitting TraceEvent.fileChanged);* a proper ancestor is not a directory (a regular file: ENOTDIR; missing: ENOENT, the CLI never creates a directory): Err, nothing changes in fs.files;* fs.files path is a directory: Err (EISDIR), nothing changes in fs.files;* otherwise (a regular file, or nothing): fs.files path := file bytes, Ok. In the three PathOk < 4096-byte cases it first appends the event TraceEvent.fileChanged p to the trace (not output; module docstring).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 347–364): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.SignalSession.readStdinHook\n\ndef writeStdoutHookL366–377 ↗#libsignal_cli_io::write_stdout(bytes: &[u8] = (x0, x1)) -> Result<(), IoError> (x0): 0-allocation C-ABI musl write(1, …) / __errno_location loop (EINTR retry, n == 0 treated as Err, and EBADF on a closed file descriptor 1 treated as Ok(()) matching std's handle_ebadf and read_stdin; in processInputs file descriptor 1 is open .stdio stdout), i.e. the engine's write(1, …), which appends the bytes to the trace as stdout events (TraceEvent.ofBytes .stdout) and leaves the file system unchanged. Ok. .Exception if the bytes are not readable.\n\nCLI-specific extern hooks\n\ndef strlenHookL381–389 ↗#strlen(s = x0) -> size_t (x0): the least n such that the bytes [s, s + n] are readable and byte s + n is 0. .Exception if there is none (a real strlen faults or runs on). Caller: CStr::from_ptr on each argument of argv, which the loader writes NUL-terminated.\n\ndef memrchrHookL391–405 ↗#core::slice::memchr::memrchr(x: u8 = w0, text: &[u8] = (x1, x2)) -> Option<usize> (x0 = tag, x1 = value): Some(i) (x0 := 1, x1 := i) for the largest i < len with text[i] = x (x: the low byte of x0), else None (x0 := 0, x1 unchanged). .Exception if the bytes are not readable. Caller: rsplit_once('.') in address (cli/src/lib.rs), which tests x0 = 1.\n\ndef Utf8OkL407–412 ↗#bs is well-formed UTF-8: the UTF-8 encoding (String.utf8EncodeChar, shortest form) of some list of Unicode scalar values (Char: no surrogates, at most U+10FFFF). This is Rust's definition (core::str::from_utf8); equivalently ∃ s : String, s.toUTF8.toList = bs up to UInt8.toBitVec.\n\ndef fromUtf8HookL414–435 ↗#core::str::converts::from_utf8(v: &[u8] = (x0, x1)) -> Result<&str, Utf8Error> (sret x8, 24 bytes): if the bytes are well-formed UTF-8 (Utf8Ok), Ok(v): tag 0 @0, x0 @8, x1 @16. Otherwise Err: tag 1 @0, then err bs = (valid_up_to, error_len), a free choice, as the word @8 and the 16-bit field @16. .Exception if the bytes are not readable or the sret not writable. (Measured on the compiled from_utf8: str xzr, [x8]; stp x0, x1, [x8, #8] and str x9(=1), [x8]; str x9, [x8, #8]; strb, strb [x8, #16..17].)\n\ndef durationSinceExactHookL437–464 ↗#<SystemTime>::duration_since(&self = x0, earlier = (x1 = tv_sec, w2 = tv_nsec)) -> Result<Duration, SystemTimeError> (sret x8), exactly std's Timespec::sub_timespec. With t1 = self (tv_sec: i64 @0, tv_nsec: u32 @8) and t2 = earlier, each read as tv_sec · 10^9 + tv_nsec nanoseconds (an integer), and d = |t1 - t2|: tag @0 = 0 (Ok) if t1 ≥ t2, else 1 (Err); the Duration (d / 10^9, d % 10^9) as secs: u64 @8 and nanos: u32 @16. .Exception if self's 12 bytes are not readable, the 24 sret bytes are not writable, or a tv_nsec is ≥ 10^9 (not a Timespec: undefined behaviour in Rust). (Measured on the compiled duration_since / sub_timespec: ldr x9, [x1]; ldr w12, [x1, #8] with self in x1 after mov x1, x9(=x0), earlier stored from x1 / w2; stp xzr, x9, [x19]; str w8, [x19, #16] for Ok, the tag XOR-ed with 1 for Err.) Every call site passes UNIX_EPOCH as earlier.\n\nThe free choices and the table\n\nstructure CliHooksL468–481 ↗#What a CLI run is quantified over (the W of cliRunOf elf W and cliRunAbortOnPanic elf W): the abstract primitives and two results the CLI cannot observe.\n\ndef cliBcmpHookL483–492 ↗#bcmp(a = x0, b = x1, n = x2) -> int: x0 := bcmp (n bytes at a) (n bytes at b), return: the function-level (C-ABI) bcmp contract with the value function bcmp. Unlike the CLI-specific hooks above it checks no permission (it does not fail closed): on unreadable bytes it over-approximates, reading them anyway, as the function-level contract does, so that the function-level proofs' bcmp facts transfer unchanged.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliRun.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliRun/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliRun"}});